mainmenu "Triplex flight computer"

config TFC_NODE_ID
	int "Flight computer node id (0 = A, 1 = B, 2 = C)"
	default 0
	range 0 2
	help
	  The same image runs on every flight computer; only this id differs (STAGED_BUILD rule 1).
	  Node 0 is the sync master at power-up; nodes 1 and 2 follow its SYNC and take over, lowest first, if it stops.

config TFC_SYNC_STAGGER_US
	int "Stagger of the SYNC wait windows between nodes (microseconds)"
	default 500
	range 200 3000
	help
	  The wait for SYNC lasts 1.0 ms plus this much for each node after A, so the lowest node's takeover SYNC is heard by the others before their own
	  windows end (ADR-025). It must exceed the receive latency of a frame. The host's native_sim CAN driver polls its socket every millisecond, so
	  the host board file lengthens it.

config TFC_VOTE_US
	int "Time of the vote within the frame (microseconds after SYNC)"
	default 7000
	range 6000 9000
	help
	  The schedule's vote slot (ARCHITECTURE section 3). The host's native_sim CAN driver polls its socket every millisecond, so with several
	  instances on one vcan0 a peer's command can reach a node up to about 2 ms after the peer sent it; the host board file moves the vote later.

config TFC_FLIGHT_FUNCTION
	bool "Compute the command with the flight function"
	default n
	help
	  On: the command is the output of the sensor consensus, the attitude estimator and the scheduled controller (core/include/tfc/flight.hpp),
	  using the pitch program and gains in flight_tables.hpp. Off (default): a scripted function of the frame number, which the Python virtual
	  peers reproduce bit for bit, so they can stand in for the other two nodes.

config TFC_ESTIMATOR_NO_ACCEL
	bool "The attitude estimator ignores the accelerometer"
	depends on TFC_FLIGHT_FUNCTION
	default n
	help
	  On: the attitude is the gyro's integral (EstimatorConfig::use_accel false), as for a vehicle that flies under thrust, where the accelerometer reads the thrust and not gravity.
	  Off (default): the accelerometer corrects the tilt while the specific force is within 0.3 g of 1 g, as on the platform on the bench. The rig builds a vehicle other than the
	  reference one with this on (tools/bench/build_vehicle_rig.py).

config TFC_LAUNCH_SEQUENCE
	bool "The launch sequence: pad calibration, mission frame, launch and scrub, go/no-go"
	depends on TFC_FLIGHT_FUNCTION
	default n
	help
	  On: each computer calibrates its own gyro on the pad (10 s at rest) and subtracts the bias before it sends; the mission frame rides in SYNC (the sync master
	  starts it on an authenticated `launch` that passes the go/no-go, and scrubs on a failure during the 10 s countdown); the schedules follow the frames since T-zero
	  and sit at their first point on the pad; the heartbeat carries a ready bit (docs/design/LAUNCH_SEQUENCE.md). Off: the schedules follow the SYNC frame number, as before.

config TFC_PHASES
	bool "Keep the mission phase and the WARM role (ADR-023)"
	default n
	help
	  On: the manager starts in the power-up phase; the authenticated `phase` command changes it (refused when fewer computers vote than the new phase's minimum), `warm` rests
	  a computer (judged by the shadow vote, not voting; `reintegrate` promotes it) and the `disable` tiers follow the phase (docs/design/MISSION_PHASES.md). Below the minimum is reported,
	  never acted on. Off: the interlock tiers of ADR-019 apply, as before.

config TFC_SENSOR_SPLIT
	bool "Judge each IMU apart from its computer (the sensor/compute split)"
	default TFC_FLIGHT_FUNCTION
	help
	  On: a bad IMU is excluded from the sensor consensus and its computer stays a voter for commands for as long as its commands agree; a bad computer is
	  excluded from the command vote and its IMU stays in the consensus (ADR-020 case 1, TS-15: option B). Off: a computer is one unit and a bad IMU latches the
	  whole computer, which is what the scripted virtual peers' fault tests expect. On by default with the flight function, which is where the consensus exists.

config TFC_RESYNC_PERIOD
	int "State resynchronisation period in frames (0: off)"
	default 100
	range 0 10000
	help
	  With the flight function on, every this many frames the computers exchange their estimator and controller state (four frames each, 0x420 to 0x42B) and
	  each adopts the mid-value of the healthy computers' states, if it has all of them (docs/design/RESYNC.md, ADR-030, TS-23). 100 is the proposed default (TS-23).
	  The manager's digest persistence is then set to twice this plus 50 frames, so that a mismatch the resync would heal is not counted. Ignored without the
	  flight function.

config TFC_RESYNC_GROUP
	hex "Computers that take part in the resynchronisation (bit n = node n)"
	default 0x07
	range 0 0x07
	help
	  The same value on every computer. A computer outside the group neither sends its state nor adopts the vote: the diverse computer of ADR-021 (the
	  golden release) is left out with 0x03 so that the resync does not erase the differences it exists to show (docs/design/RESYNC.md section 6).

config TFC_TEST_DROP_PEERS_FIRST
	int "Test knob: first frame in which this computer withholds every sensor frame from its flight function"
	default 0
	help
	  A test aid, off at 0 together with the next one. From the frame with this SYNC number, for TFC_TEST_DROP_PEERS_FRAMES frames, this computer does not give
	  any sensor frame, its peers' or its own, to its flight function (the manager still sees them), so its consensus has no trustworthy gyro, its estimator holds its
	  rates and the replicas diverge deterministically. Used by the live resynchronisation test (sim/tests/test_live_resync.py); never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_DROP_PEERS_FRAMES
	int "Test knob: how many frames the peers' sensor frames are withheld"
	default 0

config TFC_RELEASE_ID
	hex "The release this image reports in its heartbeat (0: the first four hex digits of the git hash)"
	default 0x0
	range 0 0xFFFF
	help
	  Computers on different releases are compared with the version tolerance and not blamed for differing from each other (ADR-021, docs/design/RESYNC.md section 6). The default
	  makes every image built from a different commit a different release. Set it by hand to build two releases from one tree (the live mixed-release test does).

config TFC_TEST_HANG_AT_FRAME
	int "Test knob: from this frame the end-of-frame KICK stops (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid for the supervisor's procedure (P-S2-03): from the frame with this SYNC number the node no longer raises its KICK line, while everything else
	  (frames, heartbeat, watchdog) goes on, so the supervisor has to be the one that acts. Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_T0_AT_FRAMES_TO_ZERO
	int "Test knob: the T0 line rises when this many frames remain to T-zero (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid for the launch procedure on a board without the supervisor: the line is read as high from the frame in the countdown that has this many frames left, which stands in
	  for the supervisor's edge (the sync master accepts it only in the last second, `SyncClock::kT0Window`). Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_CMD_OFFSET_MDEG
	int "Test knob: milli-degrees added to this computer's pitch command (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid (P-S3-01, the live mixed-release test): a computer whose commands are off by a known amount. Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_GYRO_BIAS_MDPS
	int "Test knob: milli-dps added to this computer's gyro x sample (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid (P-S3-01): an IMU with a known bias, sent as it is, so that the sensor consensus and the sensor split can be exercised on the real bus. Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_GYRO_DRIFT_MDPS_PER_FRAME
	int "Test knob: milli-dps added to the gyro x bias in every frame, cumulative (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid (P-S3-01): an IMU that drifts, 50 being 0.05 dps per frame. Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_TEST_CLOCK_PPM
	int "Test knob: the sync master's frame period is this many ppm long (0: off)"
	default 0
	help
	  A bench aid (P-S2-03): a master whose frame clock runs slow (positive) or fast (negative) by a known amount, for the supervisor's period check. Only the master drives the
	  period; followers lock to SYNC. Never set in a flight build.

config TFC_SIM_BUS_IMU
	bool "Take the sensor input from the simulator's frames on the bus"
	default n
	help
	  On: the node's simulated IMU reads the vehicle simulator's body rates and accelerations (ids 0x501 and 0x502, sent just after SYNC) and adds
	  its own noise, instead of the scripted motion. Only for host builds; on the board the IMU is the ISM330DHCX.

config TFC_AUTO_REINTEGRATE
	bool "Automatically readmit a node after a first, transient-looking latch"
	default n
	help
	  Off (default): a latched node is only readmitted when an operator sends a "reintegrate" ground
	  command, and only after it has passed probation (the Space Shuttle / airliner practice).
	  On: after the minimum dwell, a node whose FIRST latch had a transient-looking cause (missing or
	  damaged frames, one vote episode) goes on probation by itself. A stuck sensor, a digest
	  mismatch and a repeat offender are never readmitted automatically. See docs/decisions/DECISIONS.md ADR-010.

config TFC_GROUND_KEY
	string "Ground-command authentication key (32 hex digits = 128 bits)"
	default "000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f"
	help
	  The shared key for the SipHash-2-4 tag on ground commands (docs/decisions/DECISIONS.md ADR-019). The default is the PUBLIC
	  bench key that the host tools and the tests also use, so the bench works out of the box. It protects nothing:
	  provision your own before any real use (docs/design/FUTURE_WORK.md). The value ends up in the binary and in the build
	  directory: it is a bench secret at best, not a flight key store.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

config TFC_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Hardware watchdog timeout (ms)"
	default 100
	range 20 4000
	help
	  The watchdog is serviced once per frame, at the end of a completed frame (TFC-FDIR-038). The default is ten frames: loose
	  enough to survive a long console line on the target, tight enough to catch a hang in a tenth of a second. The requirement's
	  proposal is three frames (30 ms); tighten it once the frame time with the console is measured on the board.

config TFC_QUARANTINE_ON_RESET_LOOP
	bool "Stay silent after a reset loop"
	default y
	help
	  After TFC_RESET_LOOP_BOOTS fault resets in a row, each after a boot shorter than TFC_SHORT_BOOT_S, the node stops transmitting
	  and says so on the console once a second until it is power-cycled (TFC-FDIR-042). The others then see a silent node.

config TFC_RESET_LOOP_BOOTS
	int "Short boots in a row that make a reset loop"
	default 3
	range 1 20

config TFC_SHORT_BOOT_S
	int "A boot shorter than this many seconds is short"
	default 60
	range 1 3600
